  "transcriptText": "Big blind, with sixes. The flop comes 9, 6, 2... a set. 60 in the pot, and you act first. Lead? Or check, planning to raise?\n\nYou check. He bets 20. You raise to 70. Now he owes 50 more. 50, over 60 + 70 + 70... 50 over 200. 25%. If he calls, the pot is 200. Just calling his 20 makes it 100.\n\nBut a check-raise isn't always a monster. Same raise, with 8♠ 7♠. Fives and tens make the straight. As a bluff, it risks 70 to win 80... break-even, 46.7%. Called, it still has 8 outs of 47: 17% on the next card... 31.5% by the river. From his seat, both raises look the same.\n\nYour turn. Same flop, he bets 20, and you hold sevens. A raise folds out worse hands and keeps better ones. So just call. Check-raise your strongest hands and your best draws together. Up next: barrels and blockers."
